<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Loombre :: installers/windows/msi/Services.wxs

  ==========================================================================
  THE "ask": does a raw <ServiceInstall> pointed at node.exe work, or does
  this need a service-wrapper exe? ANSWER: a wrapper IS needed. Evidence:
  ==========================================================================

  The Windows Service Control Manager (SCM) requires the process it starts
  to call StartServiceCtrlDispatcher and register a control handler
  (SetServiceCtrlHandler) within a short timeout, then to report
  SERVICE_RUNNING via SetServiceStatus, and to respond to a later
  SERVICE_CONTROL_STOP by actually stopping and reporting SERVICE_STOPPED.
  node.exe is a plain console-subsystem executable — it implements NONE of
  that protocol. Registering `node.exe dist/main.js` directly via
  <ServiceInstall> would let SCM successfully CreateProcess it, but the
  process never calls StartServiceCtrlDispatcher, so SCM treats the start
  as failed/hung and — worse for the "clean stop" half of the ask — SCM has
  no way to deliver SERVICE_CONTROL_STOP to it at all (there is no signal
  registered to receive it), so a `net stop LoombreServer` / service-panel
  stop would fall through to SCM's hard-kill timeout on every single stop.
  This is not a Loombre-specific problem; it is exactly the reason tools
  like NSSM and WinSW exist for wrapping arbitrary executables as services.

  DECISION: a small, FULLY-OWNED wrapper (LoombreServiceHost.exe, see
  installers/windows/service-host/) is added rather than pulling in a
  third-party service-wrapper binary. Reasoning, so this isn't a silently
  added binary (the explicit instruction this file is answering):
    1. We are already building a C# tray controller for this same lane, so
       the .NET 8 toolchain is not new surface — reusing it for one more
       small project is cheap, and keeps the wrapper auditable/fully-owned
       per CLAUDE.md's "fully owned platform" posture, rather than adding
       an unsigned third-party .exe on top of Loombre's own unsigned
       posture (P4.1) — one unfamiliar unsigned binary is a strictly worse
       trust story than a second unsigned binary we wrote and can explain.
    2. It correctly implements ServiceBase (SCM protocol) AND translates a
       stop request into a CTRL_BREAK_EVENT delivered to the child's
       console (Windows' nearest equivalent to POSIX SIGTERM for a
       console-subsystem child — see LoombreHostedService.cs's header for
       the full mechanism and why AttachConsole/FreeConsole is needed), so
       `net stop LoombreServer` gets an actual graceful-shutdown attempt
       before the wrapper falls back to killing the process tree.
    3. Node.js on win32 DOES support a 'SIGBREAK' process event specifically
       for CTRL_BREAK_EVENT (see LoombreHostedService.cs) — but apps/server
       and apps/worker do not yet register a win32 SIGBREAK handler
       (STATE.md P4.14 added POSIX SIGTERM only, which Windows silently
       no-ops on an externally-delivered kill). Until that handler lands,
       Loombre's Windows services stop via the wrapper's graceful-then-kill
       TIMEOUT path (still bounded, still non-destructive to on-disk state
       for the same reasons a hard container/VM stop already has to be
       safe) rather than a true graceful drain. FLAGGED for the owning
       lane, not silently patched around here (out of this lane's
       ownership: apps/server/src, apps/worker/src).

  ==========================================================================
  THE OTHER "ask": ServiceInstall/@Arguments quoting.
  ==========================================================================

  ServiceInstall's Arguments attribute is a Windows-Installer "Formatted"
  string: [DirectoryId] tokens are resolved to the installed, ABSOLUTE,
  trailing-backslash path at install time (the same mechanism any Formatted
  MSI field uses — this is not WiX-specific magic). Because
  Program Files\Loombre contains a space, every path-shaped argument is
  wrapped in ITS OWN literal double quotes inside the Arguments value so
  LoombreServiceHost's Main(string[] args) receives each one as a single
  argv element (the .NET runtime already applies standard Windows
  command-line unquoting to what SCM hands it — see
  installers/windows/service-host/ServiceOptions.cs's header for why no
  custom quote-parsing lives on our side). XML attributes below are
  single-quoted specifically so the double quotes can be written literally
  without &quot; escaping.

  TRAILING-BACKSLASH TRAP: a bare-directory argument like "[SERVERDIR]"
  resolves to "…\server\" — and under Windows command-line rules the \"
  at the end is an ESCAPED QUOTE, so the argument swallows the closing
  quote and the rest of the line parses garbled. Every bare-directory
  argument below therefore appends a '.' inside the quotes
  ("[SERVERDIR]." -> …\server\., a valid spelling of the same directory)
  so the character before the closing quote is never a backslash.
  Arguments whose token continues with a filename ("[NODEDIR]node.exe")
  don't have the problem.
-->
<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs"
     xmlns:util="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/util">
  <?include ServerPort.wxi ?>
  <?include WebPort.wxi ?>
  <Fragment>
    <!-- Localizes to this machine's BUILTIN\Users spelling (util's
         QueryOsWellKnownSID custom action) for the per-service
         util:PermissionEx grants below — a literal "Users" account name
         would break on non-English Windows. -->
    <PropertyRef Id="WIX_ACCOUNT_USERS" />
    <ComponentGroup Id="ServiceComponents" Directory="SVCHOSTDIR">
      <!-- ONE component, THREE ServiceInstall/ServiceControl pairs: all
           three Windows services launch the SAME shared
           LoombreServiceHost.exe binary (it takes its target executable +
           args on its own command line, see Services.wxs's header +
           ServiceOptions.cs), so there is exactly one file to
           install/service/remove and the three service registrations live
           and die with it atomically. -->
      <Component Id="cmp.ServiceHost" Guid="*">
        <File Id="file.LoombreServiceHost" Source="$(var.SvcHostDir)\LoombreServiceHost.exe" KeyPath="yes" />

        <!-- START POSTURE (installer completeness audit — the end state
             the old rehearsal comment promised): the bundled node/
             payload is REAL now (build-msi.mjs's fetchNodeRuntime calls
             the shared, manifest-pinned scripts/fetch-node.mjs, staging
             node.exe at the NODEDIR root), so all three services install
             as Start="auto" and each ServiceControl carries
             Start="install" — a fresh install boots the whole stack with
             no manual `sc start`. Payload extraction still happens at
             INSTALL time (Package.wxs ca.ExtractPayload), before
             StartServices in the execute sequence, so first start only
             pays the extractor's marker-hash self-heal check.

             ORDERING: LoombreWorker and LoombreWeb declare an SCM
             ServiceDependency on LoombreServer — SCM starts the server
             first and stops it last. The worker discovers DATABASE_URL
             via LOOMBRE_DATA_DIR from the server-provisioned embedded-PG
             cluster, and the web client proxies API calls to the server,
             so both are strictly downstream. First-boot reality: the
             server reports RUNNING as soon as the wrapper spawns its
             child, but initdb + migrations take longer — the worker's
             own bounded discovery poll plus the SCM recovery actions
             below (not SCM start ordering) are what absorb that window. -->

        <!-- ==================== LoombreServer ==================== -->
        <!-- spawn-restricted (server ONLY, see ServiceOptions.cs +
             RestrictedProcess.cs): the server supervises an embedded
             postgres.exe, which hard-refuses to run from a token holding
             admin privileges — and a LocalSystem service's direct spawn
             inherits exactly such a token. The wrapper launches the child
             with a CreateRestrictedToken that disables the Administrators
             SID (pg_ctl's own technique). The worker keeps a normal token:
             it spawns ffmpeg, not postgres, and transcode file access
             relies on LocalSystem breadth. -->
        <ServiceInstall
          Id="svc.LoombreServer"
          Name="LoombreServer"
          DisplayName="Loombre Server"
          Description="Loombre media server (catalog API + playback; the web client is served by the LoombreWeb service and advertised via LOOMBRE_WEB_URL)."
          Start="auto"
          Type="ownProcess"
          ErrorControl="normal"
          Account="LocalSystem"
          Arguments='--name LoombreServer --exe "[NODEDIR]node.exe" --arg "[SERVERDIR]dist\main.js" --cwd "[SERVERDIR]." --log "[APPDATA_LOGS]server.log" --envfile "[APPDATA_DATA]server.env" --stop-timeout-ms 10000 --extract-zip "[PAYLOADDIR]payload.zip" --extract-to "[INSTALLFOLDER]." --spawn-restricted'>
          <!-- SERVICE_START (+ explicit SERVICE_QUERY_STATUS) for local
               users: the non-elevated tray's "Start server" fix. The SCM's
               default service DACL lets interactive users QUERY but not
               START — and the IPC start endpoint can never start a
               stopped server (it is hosted BY the server;
               IPC_SERVER_START_SEMANTICS), so without this grant the
               tray's only start path is a UAC prompt. SAME trust boundary
               the owner already accepted for the IPC token file
               (LOOMBRE_IPC_WINDOWS_GRANT below: any local user may drive
               status/start/stop) — this widens nothing, it makes the SCM
               agree with the IPC posture. No ServiceStop: stop stays an
               IPC-only, in-band graceful shutdown. -->
          <util:PermissionEx User="[WIX_ACCOUNT_USERS]" ServiceQueryStatus="yes" ServiceStart="yes" />
        </ServiceInstall>
        <ServiceControl
          Id="svcctl.LoombreServer"
          Name="LoombreServer"
          Start="install"
          Stop="both"
          Remove="uninstall"
          Wait="yes" />
        <!-- SCM recovery actions (installer completeness audit): restart
             on every failure tier with a 10s delay, failure count reset
             daily. Load-bearing beyond ordinary resilience — the worker's
             bounded discovery poll DEPENDS on SCM restarting it if the
             server's embedded-PG provisioning outlasts the poll window on
             a slow first boot. -->
        <util:ServiceConfig
          ServiceName="LoombreServer"
          FirstFailureActionType="restart"
          SecondFailureActionType="restart"
          ThirdFailureActionType="restart"
          RestartServiceDelayInSeconds="10"
          ResetPeriodInDays="1" />

        <!-- ==================== LoombreWorker ==================== -->
        <ServiceInstall
          Id="svc.LoombreWorker"
          Name="LoombreWorker"
          DisplayName="Loombre Worker"
          Description="Loombre background worker (scan/probe/transcode/metadata job consumers)."
          Start="auto"
          Type="ownProcess"
          ErrorControl="normal"
          Account="LocalSystem"
          Arguments='--name LoombreWorker --exe "[NODEDIR]node.exe" --arg "[WORKERDIR]dist\index.js" --cwd "[WORKERDIR]." --log "[APPDATA_LOGS]worker.log" --envfile "[APPDATA_DATA]worker.env" --stop-timeout-ms 15000 --extract-zip "[PAYLOADDIR]payload.zip" --extract-to "[INSTALLFOLDER]."'>
          <ServiceDependency Id="LoombreServer" />
          <!-- Same grant + rationale as LoombreServer's above: the tray's
               full-stack start touches all three services. -->
          <util:PermissionEx User="[WIX_ACCOUNT_USERS]" ServiceQueryStatus="yes" ServiceStart="yes" />
        </ServiceInstall>
        <ServiceControl
          Id="svcctl.LoombreWorker"
          Name="LoombreWorker"
          Start="install"
          Stop="both"
          Remove="uninstall"
          Wait="yes" />
        <util:ServiceConfig
          ServiceName="LoombreWorker"
          FirstFailureActionType="restart"
          SecondFailureActionType="restart"
          ThirdFailureActionType="restart"
          RestartServiceDelayInSeconds="10"
          ResetPeriodInDays="1" />

        <!-- ==================== LoombreWeb ==================== -->
        <!-- The apps/web Next.js standalone bundle (installer
             completeness audit — closes the "installed web files nothing
             serves" gap): build-msi.mjs stages `output: "standalone"`'s
             monorepo layout, so the entrypoint is
             [WEBDIR]apps\web\server.js and the working directory is its
             containing folder (Next resolves .next/static and public/
             relative to server.js). The cwd argument uses the same
             trailing-dot idiom as the other two services — see this
             file's header TRAILING-BACKSLASH TRAP note. No extract-zip
             pair here: the server/worker registrations already carry the
             self-heal, and one extractor owner per boot is plenty. -->
        <ServiceInstall
          Id="svc.LoombreWeb"
          Name="LoombreWeb"
          DisplayName="Loombre Web"
          Description="Loombre web client (Next.js standalone server for the browser UI; talks to the Loombre Server API)."
          Start="auto"
          Type="ownProcess"
          ErrorControl="normal"
          Account="LocalSystem"
          Arguments='--name LoombreWeb --exe "[NODEDIR]node.exe" --arg "[WEBDIR]node_modules\next\dist\bin\next" --arg start --arg --hostname --arg 0.0.0.0 --cwd "[WEBDIR]." --log "[APPDATA_LOGS]web.log" --envfile "[APPDATA_DATA]web.env" --stop-timeout-ms 10000'>
          <ServiceDependency Id="LoombreServer" />
          <!-- Same grant + rationale as LoombreServer's above. -->
          <util:PermissionEx User="[WIX_ACCOUNT_USERS]" ServiceQueryStatus="yes" ServiceStart="yes" />
        </ServiceInstall>
        <ServiceControl
          Id="svcctl.LoombreWeb"
          Name="LoombreWeb"
          Start="install"
          Stop="both"
          Remove="uninstall"
          Wait="yes" />
        <util:ServiceConfig
          ServiceName="LoombreWeb"
          FirstFailureActionType="restart"
          SecondFailureActionType="restart"
          ThirdFailureActionType="restart"
          RestartServiceDelayInSeconds="10"
          ResetPeriodInDays="1" />

        <!-- Per-service REG_MULTI_SZ Environment values use MSI's
             documented "[~]"-as-null-separator RegistryValue convention
             for Type="multiString" — no trailing "[~]" after the LAST
             entry, or MSI writes a spurious empty trailing string into
             the REG_MULTI_SZ.

             LOOMBRE_DATA_DIR=[APPDATAFOLDER], the ProgramData\Loombre
             ROOT — NOT the data\ subdirectory (installer completeness
             audit's tray-can-never-connect gap). The IPC discovery/token
             files are written at join(LOOMBRE_DATA_DIR, controller-ipc.*)
             (apps/server/src/ipc/discovery-files.ts), and the tray reads
             them from the ProgramData\Loombre root — pointing the data
             dir one level down would bury the discovery file where no
             controller looks. The embedded-PG cluster then lives at
             ProgramData\Loombre\postgres\data (provisioning appends
             postgres\data itself — see apps/server/src/bootstrap/
             provisioning.ts), which is also why Directories.wxs no longer
             ships a pgdata folder.

             Server-only entries (installer completeness audit):
             LOOMBRE_EMBEDDED_PG_VENDOR_DIR=[PGDIR] + a matching
             LOOMBRE_EMBEDDED_PG_VERSION — a packaged install has no repo
             root for the bootstrap's dev-checkout vendor default, so the
             MSI must name where it put the binaries (hard requirement per
             packages/provisioning-pg/README.md); build-msi.mjs stages the
             tree vendor-shaped (pg\windows-x64\<version>\...) and passes
             the same manifest-pinned version as the EmbeddedPgVersion
             preprocessor define, so path and version cannot drift.
             LOOMBRE_IPC_WINDOWS_GRANT grants the discovery/token files a
             read ACE for BUILTIN\Users, named by locale-independent
             well-known SID (icacls "*SID" form — the value is a
             comma-separated icacls principal list, each granted read; see
             apps/server/src/ipc/env.ts + packages/secrets/src/
             windows-acl.ts). Rationale: the tray runs as ANY logged-in
             user (HKLM Run key, Shortcuts.wxs), while the default policy
             only grants SYSTEM + Administrators.
             LOOMBRE_WEB_URL names the LoombreWeb service's URL so the IPC
             status response advertises the real web client instead of the
             server-port fallback (apps/server/src/ipc/web-url.ts).

             DATABASE_URL is deliberately ABSENT — and unlike the earlier
             revision of this comment, that is now the CORRECT, fully-wired
             posture, not an open gap: apps/server's provisioning bootstrap
             (bootstrapProvisioning) treats an unset DATABASE_URL as "run
             embedded PG" — it provisions/starts the vendored cluster under
             LOOMBRE_DATA_DIR, resolves generated credentials at boot, and
             auto-migrates; the worker reconstructs the same URL via the
             LOOMBRE_DATA_DIR discovery seam (P4.2). Baking a URL in here
             would OVERRIDE embedded mode with the external-server path.
             LOOMBRE_TRUST_PROXY stays deliberately UNSET too (README.md:
             an operator opt-in for their own trusted reverse-proxy config,
             never an installer default). -->
        <RegistryValue
          Root="HKLM"
          Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\LoombreServer"
          Name="Environment"
          Type="multiString"
          Value="NODE_ENV=production[~]PORT=$(var.ServerPort)[~]LOOMBRE_DATA_DIR=[APPDATAFOLDER][~]LOOMBRE_EMBEDDED_PG_VENDOR_DIR=[PGDIR][~]LOOMBRE_EMBEDDED_PG_VERSION=$(var.EmbeddedPgVersion)[~]LOOMBRE_IPC_WINDOWS_GRANT=*S-1-5-32-545[~]LOOMBRE_WEB_URL=http://localhost:$(var.WebPort)" />
        <RegistryValue
          Root="HKLM"
          Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\LoombreWorker"
          Name="Environment"
          Type="multiString"
          Value="NODE_ENV=production[~]LOOMBRE_DATA_DIR=[APPDATAFOLDER][~]LOOMBRE_FFMPEG=[FFMPEGDIR]ffmpeg.exe[~]LOOMBRE_FFPROBE=[FFMPEGDIR]ffprobe.exe" />
        <!-- LoombreWeb's env is pure Next.js standalone contract: PORT +
             HOSTNAME are what server.js binds (0.0.0.0 because the web UI
             is LAN-facing by the same PLAN §10 rationale as the server —
             Firewall.wxs opens the matching inbound rule);
             LOOMBRE_SERVER_ORIGIN points API proxying at the server
             service on its ServerPort.wxi port. -->
        <RegistryValue
          Root="HKLM"
          Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\LoombreWeb"
          Name="Environment"
          Type="multiString"
          Value="NODE_ENV=production[~]PORT=$(var.WebPort)[~]LOOMBRE_SERVER_ORIGIN=http://localhost:$(var.ServerPort)" />
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>
  </Fragment>
</Wix>
